  "candidates": [
    "Classify the sentiment of the following text as Positive or Negative.",
    "Classify the sentiment of the following text as Positive or Negative.\n\nGuidelines:\n1. Prioritize the overall intent and overarching valence over secondary caveats or descriptive critiques. A text that acknowledges flaws but maintains a generally positive or intrigued stance should be classified as Positive.\n2. Distinguish between comparative relief and genuine positivity. Phrases indicating a \"lack of worst-case scenario\" (e.g., \"could have been worse\") should be classified as Negative unless accompanied by an explicit positive endorsement.",
    "Classify the sentiment of the following text as either Positive or Negative.\n\nApply these decision rules:\n1. Prioritize the overall intent and final valence over secondary caveats. If a text acknowledges flaws but maintains a general stance of appreciation, curiosity, or endorsement, classify it as Positive.\n2. Distinguish between absolute positivity and comparative relief. Statements that merely suggest a situation \"could have been worse\" or \"is not a total failure\" without explicit praise must be classified as Negative.\n\nReturn only the label: Positive or Negative.",
    "Classify the sentiment of the following text as either Positive or Negative.\n\nFollow these rules:\n1. Prioritize overall intent and curiosity over secondary caveats or descriptive critiques; qualified praise remains Positive.\n2. Treat comparative relief (e.g., \"could have been worse\") as Negative unless accompanied by an explicit positive endorsement.\n3. Output only the label: Positive or Negative.",
    "Classify the sentiment of the text as Positive or Negative. \n\nWhen encountering conflicting evidence, apply these rules:\n1. Distinguish between \"qualified praise\" and negativity. If the text acknowledges flaws or gaps but maintains an overall stance of appreciation, curiosity, or intrigue, classify it as Positive. Prioritize the overarching intent over secondary caveats.\n2. Distinguish between \"comparative relief\" and actual positivity. Statements that merely suggest a situation \"could have been worse\" or avoid the worst-case scenario without providing an explicit positive endorsement must be classified as Negative.\n\nReturn only the label: Positive or Negative.",
    "Classify the sentiment of the text as Positive or Negative.\n\nFollow these guidelines:\n1. Prioritize the overarching intent and general valence over secondary caveats. If the author expresses curiosity or qualified praise despite mentioning flaws, classify as Positive.\n2. Distinguish between actual positivity and comparative relief. Statements implying a subject is \"not the worst\" or \"could have been worse\" should be classified as Negative unless accompanied by an explicit positive endorsement.",
    "Classify the sentiment of the following text as either Positive or Negative.\n\nFollow these priority rules:\n1. Overarching Intent: If the text contains qualified praise or acknowledges flaws while remaining generally intrigued or supportive, classify it as Positive. Prioritize the overall valence over secondary caveats or descriptive critiques.\n2. Comparative Relief: Do not mistake the absence of a worst-case scenario for positivity. Statements implying a subject is still poor—even if it \"could have been worse\"—must be classified as Negative unless accompanied by an explicit positive endorsement.\n\nReturn only the word \"Positive\" or \"Negative\"."
