  "diagnosis": "The errors fall into two primary semantic failure patterns:\n\n**1. Nuanced Appreciation vs. Explicit Negation**\nThe model misinterprets \"qualified praise\" as negative sentiment. When a text acknowledges flaws (e.g., conceptual gaps or pity) while maintaining an overall positive or intrigued stance, the model over-indexes on the critical adjectives and misses the overarching positive valence.\n*   **Proposed Rule:** Weight overall intent and curiosity higher than secondary caveats or descriptive critiques.\n\n**2. Comparative Relief vs. Absolute Sentiment**\nThe model fails to distinguish between a \"lack of worst-case scenario\" and actual positivity. It interprets the absence of total failure as a positive attribute, missing the inherent negativity of a statement that implies the subject is still poor, just not the worst possible version.\n*   **Proposed Rule:** Treat \"could have been worse\" or similar comparative relief as negative/neutral unless accompanied by an explicit positive endorsement.",
